<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dimitrie Cantemir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1673">1673</a>–<a href="/years/1723">1723</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dimitrie Cantemir was prince of Moldavia in 1693 and from 1710 to 1711, and a leading scholar. His alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> ended in defeat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Pruth</a> in 1711, and he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> thereafter. He wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/descriptio-moldaviae">Descriptio Moldaviae</a> and a history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> widely read in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dimitrie-cantemir.jpg" alt="Dimitrie Cantemir" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dimitrie_Cantemir_-_Portrait_from_Descriptio_Moldaviae,_1716_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dimitrie Cantemir was born in Silişteni, Moldavia, on 26 October 1673, the son of Constantin Cantemir and Ana Bantăș, a learned daughter of a local noble family. His surname is of Turkic or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> origin. In 1685 the Ottoman overlords named Constantin voivode of Moldavia. Although Constantin himself was illiterate, he educated his sons Dimitrie and Antioh thoroughly, and Dimitrie learned Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as a child. Between 1687 and 1710 he spent most of his time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> as a hostage or envoy, learning Turkish, studying Ottoman history at the Patriarchate's Greek Academy and composing Ottoman music. When Constantin died in 1693 Dimitrie briefly succeeded him but was passed over within three weeks in favour of Constantin Duca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Appointed voivode again in 1710, Cantemir believed that Ottoman Turkey was collapsing. By a secret agreement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> he placed Moldavia under Russian control and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> in his war against the Turks. The campaign ended in failure at Stănilești in July 1711, and the Cantemirs were forced into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Native voivodes of Moldavia were replaced by Greek phanariots. In 1712 Peter presented him with the Bogorodskoye District. Cantemir was made a Russian prince by Peter and a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> by Charles VI, and lived on an estate at Dmitrovka near Oryol. He died there on 21 August 1723, the day he was awarded his German title, and in 1935 his remains were returned to Iași.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Cantemir was a polyglot, speaking and writing eleven languages, and wrote as philosopher, historian, composer, musicologist, linguist, ethnographer and geographer. In 1714 he became a member of the Royal Academy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. His History of the Growth and Decay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> circulated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> for years, was printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1734 and remained the standard work on the Ottoman Empire until the middle of the 19th century; Edward Gibbon used it. Later scholarship contests many points because some of his sources were doubtful.</p><p>For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-academy-of-sciences">Berlin Academy</a> he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/descriptio-moldaviae">Descriptio Moldaviae</a> in 1714, covering geography, ethnography and economy, and drew a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> map of Moldavia which was the first real map of the country. His Chronicle of the Antiquity of the Romano-Moldavo-Wallachians asserted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> origin of the Romanian language. A Hieroglyphic History of 1705 is considered the first Romanian novel. In music he composed about forty Ottoman pieces and preserved 350 traditional instrumental works in a notation of his own in Edvar-i Musiki.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dimitrie Cantemir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later princes had less room: Petru Rareș lost his throne and the Tighina (Bender) region when Suleiman the Magnificent invaded in 1538, and <b>Dimitrie Cantemir</b>’s alliance with Peter the Great ended in the Russian defeat on the Pruth in 1711.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Silișteni, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in Dmitrovsk, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.72,24.78&amp;year=1705" aria-label="Silișteni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 583 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dimitrie Cantemir"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 853h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 853h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.72,24.78&amp;year=1705">What was here in 1705? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
